Here is a Semgrep rule that detects the vulnerable code structure in the provided patch for GenieACS:
```yaml
rules:
- id: "vuln-genieacs-7f295bee"
  pattern: |
    (
      import { domainToASCII } from "url";
      function isValidHost(host: string): boolean {
        // Valid chars in IPv4, IPv6, domain names
        if (/^[a-zA-Z0-9\-.:[\]-]+$/.test(host)) return true;

        // Check if input is an IDN convert to Punycode
        // Can't merge with above because domainToASCII doesn't accept IP addresses
        return /^[a-zA-Z0-9\-.:[\]-]+$/.test(domainToASCII(host));
      }
    )
  pattern-not: |
    (
      import { domainToASCII } from "url";
      function isValidHost(host: string): boolean {
        // Valid chars in IPv4, IPv6, domain names
        if (/^[a-zA-Z0-9\-.:[\]-]+$/.test(host)) return true;

        // Check if input is an IDN convert to Punycode
        // Can't merge with above because domainToASCII doesn't accept IP addresses
        return /^[a-zA-Z0-9\-.:[\]-]+$/.test(domainToASCII(host));
      }
    )
  languages: ["typescript"]
  message: |
    This code is vulnerable to a remote code execution attack due to the use of `domainToASCII` without proper input validation. The function converts IDNs (Internationalized Domain Names) to Punycode, which can result in a remote code execution vulnerability if the input is not properly validated.
  severity: ERROR
  metadata:
    source-url: github.com/genieacs/genieacs/commit/7f295beeecc1c1f14308a93c82413bb334045af6
    category: security
    cwe: CWE-918
    owasp: A3
    references:
      - https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/IDN_homograph_attack
```
Note that this rule is specific to the vulnerability in the provided patch and may not match other variations of the same issue. Additionally, it's important to test the rule thoroughly before using it in a production environment.
